ht olecranon with effusion. She has a family history of osteoarthritis.\n- Patient Education and Counseling: We discussed the nature of osteoarthritis and the importance of maintaining a healthy weight and using physical therapy.\n- Medical Treatment: We ordered a right elbow x-ray to assess the extent of the arthritis. We will refer her for physical therapy based on the results of the x-ray. We will prescribe meloxicam 15 mg once a day for pain and swelling.\n\n3. Psoriasis.\n- Medical Reasoning: The patient reports that her psoriasis is under control. She has been compliant with her treatment.\n- Medical Treatment: Continue clobetasol for her scalp.\n\nPatient Agreements: The patient understands and agrees with the recommended medical treatment plan.\n }}<|eot_id|>\n\n###Response B:\n{{ CHIEF COMPLAINT\n\nChronic osteoarthritis.\n\nHISTORY OF PRESENT ILLNESS\n\nCarolyn is a 49-year-old female who presents for follow-up of her chronic problems. She is here today for her osteoarthritis. She is doing okay, but she is having some issues with her osteoarthritis. It is mostly in her elbows, and it is really bothering her. She has been sitting at her desk all day at the office, and she is not sure if that is causing the increased flare-ups. She is here to discuss that today.\n\nThe patient reports that her right elbow bothers her more than her left, but she is right-handed. She reports that she is typing all day versus writing, so she is not sure if that is causing the increased pain. She reports some numbing and tingling in her hands when she is typing for long periods of time, but she tries to shake out her arms to relieve that sensation.\n\nThe patient reports that she has had a history of gout and had an episode about three months ago with inflammation of her toe. She has not had any other issues with that since we last saw her. She reports that the medication we gave her really controlled that and she has not had any other episodes.\n\nThe patient also reports that 