.\n\nThe patient reports she has been feeling tired, lacking energy, and has swollen joints in her elbows. She had an insect bite a couple of weeks ago and this is what she thinks she got Lyme disease from. She also reports body aches. She has been nauseous and has not been able to eat much, sticking to toast and crackers. She denies any high fevers. She has a bullseye rash on her abdomen on the left-hand side. She has been taking her metformin and lisinopril. Her blood pressure has been good at home. She monitors her blood pressure at home and has been staying away from salty foods. She has a blood pressure cuff at home and has been checking it regularly.\n\nREVIEW OF SYSTEMS\n\n• Constitutional: Reports fatigue, lack of energy.\n• Musculoskeletal: Reports body aches and joint pain.\n• Skin: Reports rash on the left abdomen.\n• Gastrointestinal: Reports nausea.\n\nPHYSICAL EXAMINATION\n\n• Skin: Warm to palpation, bullseye rash on the left abdomen, non-tender to palpation, no evidence of cellulitis.\n• Musculoskeletal: Pain to palpation bilaterally in the elbows.\n• Vital Signs: Within normal limits.\n\nRESULTS\n\nLyme titer is elevated.\n\nASSESSMENT AND PLAN\n\nMichelle Brown is a 31-year-old female with a past medical history of type 2 diabetes and hypertension who presents with abnormal labs.\n\nLyme disease.\n• Medical Reasoning: The patient had an insect bite 2 weeks ago and presents with symptoms consistent with Lyme disease. She had an elevated Lyme titer.\n• Additional Testing: We will order further Lyme testing.\n• Medical Treatment: We will start the patient on doxycycline 100 mg BID for 3 weeks. We will also touch base with an infectious disease doctor to make sure we are not missing anything.\n\nType 2 diabetes.\n• Medical Reasoning: The patient’s blood sugar has been stable and she will get a hemoglobin A1c to make sure we do not need to make any changes to her metformin.\n• Additional Testing: We will order a hemoglobin A1c.\n• Medical Treatment: Con