tes that her diet has been a little all over the place. The patient notes that she has been working more hours. She reports that she has had some headaches, however, she thought that it was related to her increased screen time.\n\nThe patient states that her right carpal tunnel release has been doing well. She denies numbness or tingling in her hands or fingers.\n\nREVIEW OF SYSTEMS\n\n• Cardiovascular: Endorses blood pressure issues.\n• Neurological: Endorses headaches.\n• Psychiatric: Endorses depression.\n\nPHYSICAL EXAMINATION\n\n• Neurological/Psychological: Appropriate mood and affect. Bilateral grip strength equal. Neurologically intact.\n• Neck: Supple without thyromegaly or cervical lymphadenopathy.\n• Respiratory: Lungs are clear to auscultation bilaterally. No wheezes, rales, or rhonchi.\n• Cardiovascular: Regular rate and rhythm. No murmurs, gallops, or rubs. No extra heart sounds.\n\nVITALS REVIEWED\n\n• Blood Pressure: Elevated.\n\nRESULTS\n\nLaboratory studies are all within normal limits.\n\nASSESSMENT AND PLAN\n\nChristine Flores is a 63-year-old female presenting for her yearly exam.\n\nDepression.\n• Medical Reasoning: She is doing well and managing this with medication and weekly therapy.\n• Medical Treatment: Continue current regimen.\n\nHypertension.\n• Medical Reasoning: Her blood pressures have been fluctuating lately. She does admit to dietary indiscretion due to her increased workload.\n• Medical Treatment: Increase lisinopril to 10 mg once daily.\n• Patient Education and Counseling: She should continue to monitor this at home and follow up in 1 month.\n\nCarpal tunnel release.\n• Medical Reasoning: She is doing well 2 years postoperatively and is asymptomatic at this time.\n• Patient Education and Counseling: She will contact us if her symptoms return.\n\nPatient Agreements: The patient understands and agrees with the recommended medical treatment plan.\n\n#############################\n\n\n###Response A:\n{{ CHIEF COMPLAINT\n\nRoutine check-up.\n\nHISTORY OF PRESENT ILLNESS\n\nChristine Johnson is a 63-year-old female who presents for a routine check-up.\n\nThe patient reports she has been doing well. She states she has been compliant with her medication for depression and has been seeing a therapist weekly, which has been helping her manage her symptoms. She has been sleeping 8 hours per night. She denies any suicidal ideation. She reports she has had some elevated blood pressures recently. She has been taking her medication as prescribed, but notes her diet has been all over the place lately due to increased work hours. She reports she has not had time to go grocery shopping and has been eating a lot of frozen meals. She denies any numbness or tingling in her hands. She notes some headaches, which she attributes to looking at the computer screen all day.\n\nThe patient reports that she had a carpal tunnel release done 2 years ago on the right wrist. She states she has been doing well since the surgery.\n\nREVIEW OF SYSTEMS\n\n• Constitutional: Reports sleeping 8 hours per night.\n• Eyes: Denies visual changes.\n• HENT: Denies sore throat.\n• Respiratory: Denies cough.\n• Cardiovascular: Denies chest pain.\n• Gastrointestinal: Denies abdominal pain.\n• Genitourinary: Denies dysuria.\n• Musculoskeletal: Denies joint pain.\n• Neurological: Denies headaches, denies dizziness, denies syncope.\n• Psychiatric: Denies suicidal ideation.\n\nPHYSICAL EXAM\n\n• General: Alert and oriented, in no acute distress.\n• Skin: No rashes or lesions.\n• Head: Normocephalic, atraumatic.\n• Eyes: PERRLA, EOMI, PERRL 2+, no xanthelasma.\n• Ears: TMs within normal limits, no cerumen impaction.\n• Nose: No septal deviation, no post-nasal drip.\n• Mouth: No lesions, no halitosis.\n• Neck: Supple, no lymphadenopathy, no thyromegaly.\n• Respiratory: Lungs are clear to auscultation bilaterally. No wheezes, rales, or rhonchi.\n• Cardiovascular: Regular rate and rhythm, no murmurs, no extra heart sounds.\n• Abdomen: Soft, non-tender, no hepatosplenomegaly.\n• Extremities: No edema, no clubbing or cyanosis.\n• Neurological: Cranial nerves II-XII intact, normal strength in all extremities, normal sensation, no focal deficits.\n\nRESULTS\n\nBlood pressure today is 145/90 mmHg.\n\nLabs: All within normal limits.\n\nASSESSMENT AND PLAN\n\nThe patient is a 63-year-old female who presents for a routine check-up.\n\nHypertension.\n• Medical Reasoning: Her blood pressure today was elevated.\n• Medical Treatment: Increase lisinopril to 10 mg once a day.\n\nDepression.\n• Medical Reasoning: She is doing well with her current treatment.\n• Medical Treatment: Continue current treatment regimen.\n\nCarpal Tunnel Syndrome.\n• Medical Reasoning: She reports doing well.\n• Medical Treatment: Continue current treatment regimen.\n\nPatient Agreements: The patient understands and agrees with the recommended medical treatment plan.\n\nINSTRUCTIONS\n\nThe patient will follow up in 1 month. }}<|eot_id|>\n\n###Response B:\n{{ CHIEF COMPLAINT\n\nRoutine annual physical exam.\n\nHISTORY OF PRESENT ILLNESS\n\nChristine is a 63-year-old female who presents for a routine annual physical exam. She has a history of hypertension and depression. She reports that her depression has been well-controlled with the medication she has been taking and she has been attending therapy weekly. She reports that she has been getting a full eight hours of sleep per night. She denies any suicidal ideations. She has had hypertension in the past and reports that her blood pressure has been all over the place lately, with readings that have been both high and low. She has been taking her medication as prescribed. She reports that she has been eating a lot of frozen meals and has not had time to cook. She denies any headaches or swelling. She has a history of carpal tunnel syndrome and reports that her symptoms have been doing well.\n\nREVIEW OF SYSTEMS\n\nMusculoskeletal: Reports numbness and tingling in her hands. She denies any numbness