k, but also stated he consumed several cups of coffee on an empty stomach the morning of. He denies additional episodes of palpitations.\n\nHe has been monitoring his blood pressure at home daily, obtaining systolic blood pressure readings of 146 to 155. He does report a family history of hypertension. Additionally, he has been monitoring his salt intake and feels he is doing well at reducing it. He also walks on his treadmill as often as he can.\n\nHe has a new primary care physician who started him on a new medication Benicar HCT, which he has been using for the past month.\n\nPAST HISTORY\n\nMedical\nHypertension.\n\nFAMILY HISTORY\n\nSeveral family members positive for hypertension.\n\nCURRENT MEDICATIONS\n\nBenicar HCT 40 mg/25 mg\nAmlodipine 10 mg daily.\n\nPhysical Examination\n\nCardiovascular\nNormal rate, regular rhythm, S2 normal, normal heart sounds, and normal pulses.\n\nPulmonary/Chest\nBreath sounds normal. \n\nAbdominal\nThere is no tenderness.\n\nMusculoskeletal\nThere is no edema.\n\nRESULTS\n\nThyroid profile 03/01/2021.\n\nASSESSMENT\n\n• Resistant hypertension.\n\nMr. Roberts presents with resistant hypertension, with systolic blood pressure readings between 146-155. He is currently on maximal doses for 3 medications.\n\nPLAN\n\nI have added spironolactone to his regimen which will require monitoring of his potassium and sodium. We will schedule him for a follow up in 2 weeks to complete a basic metabolic panel and check his blood pressure. Next medication to consider adding is carvedilol. I have tried to avoid this, given the potential for issues with erectile dysfunction. I counseled the patient about spironolactone side effects, including gynecomastia and electrolyte disturbances, as well as his increased risk of dehydration, with one sign to watch for being dizziness. If we are not able to improve his blood pressure with spironolactone, the next step would be sending him to the resistant hypertension clinic.\n\nI provided and reviewed with Mr. Roberts the American Heart association website (heart.org) and encouraged him to access for tips on lowering his salt intake and increasing his potassium intake. His maximum salt intake should be 1500 to <2000 mg per day. He was provided materials to follow the DASH diet and encouraged to meet 30-min of walking most days.\n\nINSTRUCTIONS\n\nSchedule follow up appointment in 2 weeks for blood pressure check and basic metabolic panel. Reduce sodium intake to 1500 to <2000 mg per day. Follow DASH diet.\n\n#############################\n\n\n###Response A:\n{{ CHIEF COMPLAINT\n\nHypertension and palpitations.\n\nHISTORY OF PRESENT ILLNESS\n\nJordan Roberts is a 49-year-old male who presents to the clinic today for the management of hypertension. He was recently seen in the emergency room for elevated blood pressure and palpitations. The patient states he has a history of hypertension. He has been feeling fine and has not experienced any episodes of palpitations since his last visit.\n\nThe patient reports that he has had issues with hypertension since he was young. He states he has a family history of hypertension. He states he thought his blood pressure was better and he has a new primary care physician who placed him on a new medication one month ago.\n\nThe patient is currently taking Lisinopril 10 mg daily and Benicar HCT 20-5 mg daily. He is taking his blood pressure daily and reports his blood pressure has been running 146-155. He is trying to limit his salt intake and is trying to walk on the treadmill when he can.\n\nGENDER\n\nMale.\n\nAGE\n\n49.\n\nDATE OF BIRTH\n\n03/02/1972.\n\nSOCIAL HISTORY\n\nPatient reports he is married with two children. He is a truck driver and works night shifts. He is a college graduate and enjoys walking on the treadmill and playing video games.\n\nFAMILY HISTORY\n\nPositive for hypertension.\n\nCURRENT MEDICATIONS\n\nLisinopril 10 mg daily.\nBenicar HCT 20-5 mg daily.\n\nPHYSICAL EXAM\n\nHeart: Regular rate and rhythm.\nLungs: Clear to auscultation bilaterally.\n\nRESULTS\n\nPotassium 4.0 mmol/L, 03/01/2021.\n\nASSESSMENT\n\nResistant hypertension.\n\nPLAN\n\nI have added spironolactone to his regimen. We will need to monitor his potassium and sodium. He will have a metabolic panel and blood pressure check in 2 weeks. Next medication would be carvedilol, which I would like to avoid giving, it\'s potential for erectile dysfunction. I have talked to the patient about that and the potential side effects of spironolactone, including the gynecomastia and electrolyte disturbances. If he is not controlled on spironolactone, the next step would be sending him to a resistant hypertension clinic.\n\nINSTRUCTIONS\n\nThe patient will follow up in 2 weeks for a blood pressure check and metabolic panel. }}<|eot_id|>\n\n###Response B:\n{{ CHIEF COMPLAINT\n\nResistant hypertension.\n\nHISTORY OF PRESENT ILLNESS\n\nMr. Jordan Roberts is a 49-year-old male who presents to the clinic for follow-up of hypertension and palpitations. He reports that he has been experiencing palpitations and hypertension since he was young. He has a family history of hypertension. He states that he was recently seen in the emergency department and was placed on a new medication called Benicar HCT. He states that he has been taking his medication as directed. He denies any chest pain, shortness of breath, or palpitations since his last visit.\n\nThe patient states that he has been having issues with his blood pressure since he was young. He reports that he has had to take medication for his blood pressure for many years. He states that he has been trying to get his blood pressure under control, but it has been difficult. He reports that he has been checking his blood pressure at home and it has been running around 146-155 mmHg.\n\nThe patient reports that he has been trying to reduce his salt intake. He states that he has been walking on the treadmill and has been doing pretty well with his exercise.\n\nRESULTS\n\nVitals: Blood Pressure: 146/80 mmHg.\n\nREVIEW OF SYSTEMS\n\n• Cardiovascular: Denies chest pain or shortness of breath.\n• Respiratory: Denies shortness of breath.\n• Gastrointestinal: Denies abdominal pain.\n• Musculoskeletal: Denies muscle weakness.\n• Neurological: Denies headaches.\n• Endocrine: Denies polyuria.\n\nPHYSICAL EXAMINATION\n\nConstitutional: No signs of jaundice or clubbing. No lymphadenopathy. No petechiae.\nCardiovascular: Heart rate regular without arrhythmias. Auscultation of the heart reveals a regular rate and rhythm without murmurs, gallops, or rubs. Auscultation of the lungs reveals clear to bronchial breath sounds bilaterally.\nMusculoskeletal: No edema. No muscle weakness.\n\nASSESSMENT AND PLAN\n\nHypertension\n\nThe patient presents with resistant hypertension. He is currently on three medications at maximum dose. I have added Spironolactone to his regimen. We will need to monitor his potassium and sodium. He will have a metabolic panel and blood pressure check in two weeks. If he is not controlled on Spironolactone, the next step would be sending him to the resistant hypertension clinic. I have talked to the patient about the potential side effects of Spironolactone including gynecomastia and electrolyte disturbances. He is advised to hydrate and to monitor his electrolytes. I have given him a handout on the DASH eating plan.\n\nPatient Education\n\nThe patient was given a handout on the DASH eating plan.\n\nPatient Agreements\n\nThe patient understands the treatment plan and agrees to follow the plan.\n\nPatient Agreements\n\nThe patient understands the potential side effects of Spironolactone and agrees to report any side effects to his provider.\n\nINSTRUCTIONS\n\nThe patient is instructed to follow up in two weeks. }}<|eot_id|>\n\n###Reference Answer:\nCHIEF COMPLAINT\n\nHigh blood pressure and palpitations.\n\nHISTORY OF PRESENT ILLNESS\n\nJordan Roberts is a 49-year-old with a long history of hypertension and an acute episode of palpitations.\n\