r. Roberts the American Heart association website (heart.org) and encouraged him to access for tips on lowering his salt intake and increasing his potassium intake. His maximum salt intake should be 1500 to <2000 mg per day. He was provided materials to follow the DASH diet and encouraged to meet 30-min of walking most days.\n\nINSTRUCTIONS\n\nSchedule follow up appointment in 2 weeks for blood pressure check and basic metabolic panel. Reduce sodium intake to 1500 to <2000 mg per day. Follow DASH diet.\n\n#############################\n\n\n###Response A:\n{{ CHIEF COMPLAINT\n\nHypertension and palpitations.\n\nHISTORY OF PRESENT ILLNESS\n\nJordan Roberts is a 49-year-old male who presents to the clinic today for the management of hypertension. He was recently seen in the emergency room for elevated blood pressure and palpitations. The patient states he has a history of hypertension. He has been feeling fine and has not experienced any episodes of palpitations since his last visit.\n\nThe patient reports that he has had issues with hypertension since he was young. He states he has a family history of hypertension. He states he thought his blood pressure was better and he has a new primary care physician who placed him on a new medication one month ago.\n\nThe patient is currently taking Lisinopril 10 mg daily and Benicar HCT 20-5 mg daily. He is taking his blood pressure daily and reports his blood pressure has been running 146-155. He is trying to limit his salt intake and is trying to walk on the treadmill when he can.\n\nGENDER\n\nMale.\n\nAGE\n\n49.\n\nDATE OF BIRTH\n\n03/02/1972.\n\nSOCIAL HISTORY\n\nPatient reports he is married with two children. He is a truck driver and works night shifts. He is a college graduate and enjoys walking on the treadmill and playing video games.\n\nFAMILY HISTORY\n\nPositive for hypertension.\n\nCURRENT MEDICATIONS\n\nLisinopril 10 mg daily.\nBenicar HCT 20-5 mg daily.\n\nPHYSICAL EXAM\n\nHeart: Regular rate and rhythm.\nLungs: Cl