essure: 146/80 mmHg.\n\nREVIEW OF SYSTEMS\n\n• Cardiovascular: Denies chest pain or shortness of breath.\n• Respiratory: Denies shortness of breath.\n• Gastrointestinal: Denies abdominal pain.\n• Musculoskeletal: Denies muscle weakness.\n• Neurological: Denies headaches.\n• Endocrine: Denies polyuria.\n\nPHYSICAL EXAMINATION\n\nConstitutional: No signs of jaundice or clubbing. No lymphadenopathy. No petechiae.\nCardiovascular: Heart rate regular without arrhythmias. Auscultation of the heart reveals a regular rate and rhythm without murmurs, gallops, or rubs. Auscultation of the lungs reveals clear to bronchial breath sounds bilaterally.\nMusculoskeletal: No edema. No muscle weakness.\n\nASSESSMENT AND PLAN\n\nHypertension\n\nThe patient presents with resistant hypertension. He is currently on three medications at maximum dose. I have added Spironolactone to his regimen. We will need to monitor his potassium and sodium. He will have a metabolic panel and blood pressure check in two weeks. If he is not controlled on Spironolactone, the next step would be sending him to the resistant hypertension clinic. I have talked to the patient about the potential side effects of Spironolactone including gynecomastia and electrolyte disturbances. He is advised to hydrate and to monitor his electrolytes. I have given him a handout on the DASH eating plan.\n\nPatient Education\n\nThe patient was given a handout on the DASH eating plan.\n\nPatient Agreements\n\nThe patient understands the treatment plan and agrees to follow the plan.\n\nPatient Agreements\n\nThe patient understands the potential side effects of Spironolactone and agrees to report any side effects to his provider.\n\nINSTRUCTIONS\n\nThe patient is instructed to follow up in two weeks. }}<|eot_id|>\n\n###Reference Answer:\nCHIEF COMPLAINT\n\nHigh blood pressure and palpitations.\n\nHISTORY OF PRESENT ILLNESS\n\nJordan Roberts is a 49-year-old with a long history of hypertension and an acute episode of palpitations.\n\