consumption . i have asked her to follow up in one month by emailing over mychart regarding any side effects and issues , or issues with elavil or if adjustments are needed .\n#############################\n\nHere is the ground truth note:\n#############################\nCHIEF COMPLAINT\n\nFollow up irritable bowel syndrome (IBS).\n\nHISTORY OF PRESENT ILLNESS\n\nMs. Brittany Edwards is a pleasant 32-year-old female who returns in follow up for irritable bowel syndrome (IBS) with functional abdominal pain, nausea, and vomiting.\n\nMs. Edwards was last seen in August 2019 with flares of abdominal pain and was diagnosed with irritable bowel syndrome that was treated with Bentyl. She had a previous prescription for Reglan that she received from the emergency room that she used as needed for nausea. She was instructed to start a low dose fiber supplement such as Citrucel daily, probiotics to help with gas and bloating, and Bentyl up to 4 times daily for intestinal cramping. She was instructed to discontinue Reglan and to use Zofran as needed for nausea. Neuromodulators such as Elavil and Buspar were discussed but not started.\n\nMs. Edwards continues to have intermittent abdominal symptoms. Monday through Thursday she has less flare ups of her symptoms but states she is currently having cramps and sharp pains. She describes she recently ate shrimp which caused her to have both upper and lower abdominal pain and bloating. However, she notices episodes of diarrhea after eating chicken alfredo and consuming alcohol. She does not believe the alfredo contributes to her symptoms. The patient states she consumes 2 drinks when out of either tequila or brown liquor and occasionally a martini. She does her best to avoid “heavy foods,” but ate fried chicken today without any symptoms. She has stopped drinking sodas which has been helpful.\u202fThe patient reports passing 2-3 formed stools a day.\n\nREVIEW OF SYMPTOMS\n\n•\u202fGastrointestinal:\u202fPositive nausea, diarrhea, and abdominal pain.\n\nPHYSICAL EXAM\n\nAlert and cooperative, appears stated age.\n\nRespiratory\nLungs clear to auscultation bilaterally.\n\nCardiovascular\nHeart regular rate and rhythm.\n\nGastrointestinal\nAbdomen soft, non-tender, no masses or organomegaly. Normoactive bowel sounds.\n\nASSESSMENT\n\n• Irritable bowel syndrome\n\nBrittany Edwards is a 32-year-old established patient who returned today to discuss management of IBS and functional abdominal pain made worse by high stress, poor eating habits, and poor sleeping habits.\n\nPLAN\n\nI have encouraged dietary and lifestyle modifications as well as starting a neuromodulator, such as Elavil 25 mg at bedtime for IBS. She can use\u202fBentyl\u202f20 mg as needed 30-45 minutes\u202fbefore eating out at a restaurant. She can also use Zofran as needed for nausea. However, I have encouraged her to reduce greasy fatty foods and alcohol consumption.\n\nINSTRUCTIONS\n\nSend MyChart message in 1 month regarding any side effects of issues with Elavil or if dose adjustments are needed.\nReturn to clinic in 1 month or sooner if needed.\n#############################\n\n\n###Response A:\n{{ CHIEF COMPLAINT\n\nReturn visit for IBS with functional abdominal pain, nausea, and vomiting.\n\nHISTORY OF PRESENT ILLNESS\n\nBrittany Edwards is a 32-year-old female who was last seen in August of 2019 with flares of abdominal pain who was diagnosed with IBS. She was treated with Bentyl. She had a previous prescription for Reglan that she received from the emergency room which she used as needed for nausea. She was instructed to start a low dose fiber supplement, such as Citrucel, daily, probiotics to help with gas and bloating, Bentyl up to four times daily for intestinal cramping, and to stop Reglan and use Zofran as needed. Neuromodulators such as Elavil and Buspar were discussed but not started.\n\nThe patient reports that she has been going out to eat on Friday nights and Saturday nights and has had some spicy foods or spicy shrimp. Her gut does not always appreciate these foods.\n\nShe has been experiencing cramping and nausea. She states she is starting to notice bowel movements between 2-3 times a day, and they are formed stools. She tends to have diarrhea after she goes out to eat. She does not think it is the food or alcohol, but rather the combination. She does not think the pasta bothers her. She does not think the alcohol bothers her either. She prefers to drink Patron or dark liquor. She drinks 2 drinks every now and then. She does not drink a martini often. She does not think the mixer bothers her.\n\nWhen she is not eating out or drinking, what she does on a daily basis does not keep her regular. When she does flare up, it is not as bad. She has not used Citrucel regularly. She is still having cramping and sharp pains, so she is avoiding heavy foods. She did have some fried chicken today. She did not have any problems.\n\nThe patient states that she cannot go without fried chicken at least once in a while.\n\nPAST HISTORY\n\nMedical\nIrritable Bowel Syndrome\n\nSOCIAL HISTORY\n\nShe enjoys going out to eat on Friday nights and Saturday nights.\n\nMEDICATIONS\n\nBentyl 20 mg as needed 30-45 minutes prior to eating out at a restaurant.\n\nPHYSICAL EXAM\n\nRespiratory\nLungs are clear to auscultation bilaterally.\n\nCardiovascular\nRegular rate and rhythm.\n\nGastrointestinal\nThe abdomen is soft, non-tender. No masses or organomegaly. Normal active bowel sounds.\n\nASSESSMENT\n\nIrritable Bowel Syndrome (IBS) and functional abdominal pain, aggravated by high stress, poor eating habits, and poor sleeping habits.\n\nPLAN\n\nI have encouraged dietary and lifestyle modifications. I have also encouraged her to start a neuromodulator such as Elavil 25 mg at bedtime for IBS. She can use Bentyl 20 mg as needed 30-45 minutes prior to eating out at a restaurant. She can also use Zofran if needed. I have encouraged her to reduce greasy and fatty foods and alcohol consumption. I have asked her to follow up in one month by email over MyChart regarding any side effects and issues with Elavil or if adjustments are needed.\n\nINSTRUCTIONS\n\nWe discussed the diagnosis of IBS today. All of her questions were answered. }}<|eot_id|>\n\n###Response B:\n{{ CHIEF COMPLAINT\n\nIBS with functional abdominal pain, nausea, vomiting.\n\nHISTORY OF PRESENT ILLNESS\n\nMs. Brittany Edwards is a pleasant 32-year-old female who was last seen in August of 2019 with flares of abdominal pain, who was diagnosed with irritable bowel syndrome (IBS) and was treated with Bentyl.\n\nShe reports that she has been going out to eat on Friday and Saturday nights and has been having spicy foods or spicy shrimp. She states that her gut does not appreciate this. She states that she has been having nausea and abdominal pain.\n\nShe reports that she has been having bowel movements between 2-3 times a day. She states that they are formed. She states that after she has gone out and eaten with her friends, she tends to have more diarrhea. She states that she usually eats pasta, the chicken alfredo, at Friday\'s restaurant. She states that it usually does not bother her. She states that she drinks tequila or dark liquor. She states that she prefers Patron, but if she gets brown liquor, she only gets two. She states that she will sometimes drink a martini.\n\nShe states that she has not been using the fiber supplement, Citrucel, regularly. She states that she needs to find a new drink.\n\nShe reports that she is still having cramping and sharp pains, so she has been avoiding heavy foods. She states that she did have some fried chicken today, though. She states that she is still having the cramping and sharp pains, so she has been avoiding heavy foods. She states that she did have some fried chicken today, though. She states that she is still having the cramping and sharp pains, so she has been avoiding heavy foods. She states that she did have some fried chicken today, though.\n\nShe states that 