or mood swings.\n\nVITALS\n\nBlood pressure 124/80, heart rate is 64, respiratory rate is 18, weight 174 pounds, and pain is 0/10.\n\nPHYSICAL EXAM\n\nConstitutional\nMr. Watson is a pleasant and cooperative man who is able to converse easily though his daughter adds some details.\n\nHead and Face\nThe shunt site was clean, dry, and intact and confirmed at a setting of 2.0.\n\nNeurological\nMental status: Tested for recent and remote memory, attention span, concentration, and fund of knowledge. He scored 26/30 on the MMSE when tested with spelling and 25/30 when tested with calculations. Of note, he was able to get two of the three memory words with cuing and the third one with multiple choice. This was a slight improvement over his initial score of 23/30 with calculations and 24/30 with spelling and at that time he was unable to remember any memory words with cuing and only one with multiple choice.\nGait: Tested using the Tinetti assessment tool. He was tested without an assistive device and received a gait score of 6-8/12 and a balance of score of 12/16 for a total score of 18-20/28. This has slightly improved from his initial score of 15-17/28.\nCranial Nerves: Pupils are equal. Extraocular movements are intact. Face symmetric. No dysarthria.\nMotor: Normal for bulk and strength.\nCoordination: Slow for finger-to-nose.\n\nRESULTS\n\nCT scan of the head from 10/15/2020: It shows a frontal horn span at the level of foramen of Munro of 4.6 cm with a 3rd ventricular contour that is flat with the span of 10 mm. By my reading, there is a tiny amount of blood in the right frontal region with just a tiny subdural collection. This was not noticed by the radiologist who stated no extra axial fluid collections. There is also substantial small vessel ischemic change.\n\nASSESSMENT\n\n• Adult hydrocephalus.\n• Gait impairment.\n• Urinary incontinence and urgency.\n• Cognitive impairment.\n\nMr. Watson has made some improvement with his hydrocephalus since shunt surgery. There is a tiny fluid collection in the right parietal region. I do not believe that this tiny amount of fluid is symptomatic, and it was not documented by the radiologist when he read the CT scan.\n\nMr. Watson asked me about whether he will be able to drive again. Unfortunately, I think it is unlikely that his speed of movement will improve to a level that he will be able to pass a driver\'s safety evaluation, however, occasionally patients surprise me by improving enough over 9 to 12 months that they are able to pass such a test. I would certainly be happy to recommend such a test if I believe he is likely to pass it and he is always welcome to enroll in a driver\'s safety program without my recommendation, however, I think it is exceeding unlikely that he has the capability of passing this rigorous test at this time. I also think it is quite likely he will not regain sufficient speed of motion to pass such a test.\n\nPLAN\n\nI had a long discussion with the patient and his daughter. We are all pleased that he has started to make some improvement with his hydrocephalus because of the fluid collection in the right parietal region, I would like to leave the setting at 2.0 for another three months before we consider changing the shunt.\n\nINSTRUCTIONS\n\nFollow up in 3 months.\n#############################\n\n\n###Response A:\n{{ CHIEF COMPLAINT\n\nFollow-up for adult hydrocephalus.\n\nHISTORY OF PRESENT ILLNESS\n\nChristopher Watson is a pleasant and cooperative 86-year-old male who presents to the clinic today with his daughter, Theresa. He has a history of progressive gait impairment, cognitive impairment, and decreased bladder control for the past 8-10 years. He was started on a spinal catheter protocol in June 2020 and underwent shunt surgery on 08/01/2020. A Medtronic Strata programmable shunt in the ventricular peritoneal configuration was placed and programmed at a level 2.0.\n\nThe patient reports that he has not been hospitalized since his shunt surgery 3 months ago. He notes that his walking has improved, and he is now able to walk to the dining room without assistance. He still has times where he requires help. He reports that his bladder control has improved but is now stable and he is wearing diapers. He denies any issues with sores or decreased/painful urination. He reports that his cognitive abilities have also improved and he no longer feels "lost" in conversations.\n\nThe patient denies any headaches or pain from the shunt. He also denies any abdominal pain.\n\nPAST HISTORY\n\nMedical\nHydrocephalus.\n\nSOCIAL HISTORY\n\nRetired teacher.\n\nFAMILY HISTORY\n\nDaughter.\n\nMEDICATIONS\n\nSpinal catheter protocol.\n\nREVIEW OF SYSTEMS\n\nConstitutional: Denies fever or unintentional weight loss.\nGenitourinary: Endorses urinary incontinence and wearing a diaper. Denies sores, decreased or painful output.\nNeurological: Endorses walking impairment and cognitive impairment. Denies headaches, recent falls, or hallucinations.\nPsychiatric: Denies depression, excessive worrying, or mood swings.\n\nVITALS\n\nBlood pressure: 124/80\nHeart rate: 64\nRespirations: 18\nWeight: 174 pounds\nPain: 0/10\n\nPHYSICAL EXAM\n\nNeurological\nMental Status Exam: MMSE: 26/30 (spelling 25/30, fund of knowledge 26/30). Slight improvement from initial score of 23/30 (spelling) and 24/30 (calculations). Able to get 2 of 3 memory words with cuing, and the third with multiple choice. Previous test showed unable to remember any memory words with cuing without multiple choice and only one with multiple choice.\nTinetti Assessment Tool: Gait score: 6-8/12 without assistive device, balance score: 12/16, total score: 18/20-20/28. Slightly improved from initial score of 15-17/28.\nCranial nerves: Pupils equal. EOMI. Face symmetric. No dysarthria. Motor: Normal bulk and strength. Coordination: Slow finger to nose.\n\nRESULTS\n\nCT of the head 10/15/2020 shows a frontal horn span at the level of the foramen of Monro of 4.6 cm with a third ventricular contour that is flat with a span of 10 mm. By my reading, there is a tiny amount of blood in the right frontal region with a tiny subdermal collection. This was not noticed or noted by the radiologist who stated any extra-axial fluid collections. There is also substantial small vessel ischemic change.\n\nASSESSMENT AND PLAN\n\nAdult hydrocephalus.\nGait impairment.\nUrinary incontinence and urgency.\nCognitive impairment.\n\nThe patient remains stable on his current shunt settings. The CT scan from 10/15/2020 shows a tiny amount of blood in the right frontal region with a tiny subdermal collection. I do not believe this is causing symptoms and it was not noted by the radiologist.\n\nI recommend leaving the shunt as is for another 2-3 months. We will re-evaluate at that time. The patient has improved significantly and it is unlikely that his movement speed will improve to the level needed to pass a driver safety evaluation. He may enroll in a driver safety program without my recommendation and we can continue to watch for improvement.\n\nAll questions were answered. }}<|eot_id|>\n\n###Response B:\n{{ CHIEF COMPLAINT\n\nAdult Hydrocephalus\n\nHISTORY OF PRESENT ILLNESS\n\nMr. Watson is an 86-year-old male who returns in follow-up for adult hydrocephalus. This is his first visit since undergoing shunt surgery on August 1st, 2020.\n\nThe patient is accompanied by his daughter, Theresa.\n\nThe patient reports he has not been sick or hospitalized since the surgery. He has been doing well and has improved walking, thinking, and bladder control.\n\nThe patient\'s daughter reports that the patient is able to walk to the dining room without assistance, which is an improvement. However, he still has times when she needs to help him. She also reports that his bladder control has improved, but has leveled off and he now wears diapers. There are no issues with sores from the diaper and no decreased or painful urination.\n\nThe patient reports that his thinking is better and he 