itis is well-controlled, although it has not fully resolved. The patient was applying doxycycline twice a day with improvement, but her pharmacist advised her to reduce to once daily since it had started to improve; therefore, for approximately the past week, she has been using the doxycycline only once per day. She has not seen continued improvement but it has not worsened. She is using the sulfacetamide wash twice daily, denies drying of the skin, and reports that her skin is much improved moisturization. She also applies MetroCream immediately after using the sulfacetamide wash. She denies using other lotions and products, Cetaphil lotion or soap, or Neutrogena wipes. The patient did not begin Elidel because it is not covered by her insurance, and she was concerned about possible side effects from the medication.\n\nPAST HISTORY\n\nMedical\nPerioral dermatitis.\n\nCURRENT MEDICATIONS\n\nDoxycycline cream. Apply twice daily.\nMetroCream. \nSulfacetamide wash. Apply topically twice daily.\n\nPHYSICAL EXAM\n\nIntegumentary\nSkin inspection of the face was performed today. On bilateral medial cheeks, there are a few, very faint, erythematous papules with mild redness around and underneath the nostrils. Fitzpatrick skin type is 4 to 5.\n\nASSESSMENT\n\n• Perioral dermatitis\n• Rosacea, including ocular rosacea\n\nJacqueline Miller is a 40-year-old female who presented today for follow-up for her perioral dermatitis, which has improved but not resolved. She decreased her doxycycline per her pharmacist’s recommendation, which possibly stagnated the resolution of symptoms.\n\nPLAN\n\nPerioral dermatitis.\nI counseled the patient about the risks and benefits of doxycycline and recommended she resume twice daily application until she reaches full resolution, and at that point she should reduce application to once a day for an additional week. The patient will not be using Elidel as I reviewed the cost of the medication and without insurance, it was too expensive. I provided a prescription for Tacrolimus and counseled her on possible side effects of using the medication. She will continue with Sulfacetamide face wash once a day. Also, use Bruder mask as needed.\n\nRosacea, including ocular rosacea.\nContinue using MetroCream twice daily. I recommended to gently wash her eyelids once daily with a gentle cleanser and try a gentle cream such as Vanicream.\n\nINSTRUCTIONS\n\nCall the clinic if symptoms worsen. The patient will have a follow-up appointment in 4 weeks.\n#############################\n\n\n###Response A:\n{{ CHIEF COMPLAINT\n\nFollow-up for rosacea.\n\nHISTORY OF PRESENT ILLNESS\n\nJacqueline Miller is a 32-year-old female who presents for follow-up for rosacea.\n\nThe patient reports that she has been using sulfacetamide wash twice daily and metronidazole cream after washing. She has also been using doxycycline 100 mg once daily for the past week. She has been taking doxycycline for the past 2 weeks, and she noticed improvement. However, she stopped breastfeeding and asked for doxycycline at that time. She has plateaued on improvement with the doxycycline once daily, so she started taking it twice daily again. She read that doxycycline could be problematic and stopped using the elidel cream. She has been using doxycycline once daily for the past week and is not breastfeeding.\n\nThe patient reports that she has fitzpatrick skin type IV-V. She has erythematous papules on her bilateral medial cheeks. There is mild erythema around and beneath her nostrils. She is using doxycycline twice daily and sulfacetamide wash twice daily. She is not using cetaphil cleanser, neutrogena wipes, or lotion. She is not using elidel cream.\n\nThe patient is advised to continue doxycycline twice daily until her rash is resolved, then once daily for another week before stopping. She is also advised to continue sulfacetamide wash once daily and metronidazole cream after washing.\n\nASSESSMENT AND PLAN\n\n1. Perioral dermatitis.\n• Medical Reasoning: The patient has plateaued on doxycycline once daily.\n• Patient Education and Counseling: We discussed that doxycycline is low risk. I advised that she continue doxycycline twice daily until her rash is resolved, then once daily for another week before stopping.\n• Medical Treatment: Continue doxycycline 100 mg twice daily until the rash resolves, then once daily for another week before stopping. Continue sulfacetamide wash once daily. Continue metronidazole cream after washing.\n\n2. Rosacea.\n• Medical Reasoning: The patient has been using sulfacetamide wash and metronidazole cream.\n• Patient Education and Counseling: We discussed that she should continue using sulfacetamide wash once daily. She can continue metronidazole cream after washing. I advised that she avoid any anti-aging or plant-based products. She should reach out if she tries any new products.\n• Medical Treatment: Continue sulfacetamide wash once daily. Continue metronidazole cream after washing.\n\nINSTRUCTIONS\n\nThe patient will follow up in 4 weeks. She will reach out if she starts to flare. I will give her samples of vanicream.\n\nPatient Agreements: The patient understands and agrees with the recommended medical treatment plan. }}<|eot_id|>\n\n###Response B:\n{{ CHIEF COMPLAINT\n\nRash on face.\n\nHISTORY OF PRESENT ILLNESS\n\nMs. Miller is a 44-year-old female who presents for follow-up for a rash on her face. She reports that she is doing well and her rash is much better. She states that she started taking doxycycline one a day and it plateaued. She states that she is not breastfeeding and was taking the doxycycline and using the sulfacetamide wash twice a day. She notes that she used to use the cetaphil cleanser and neutrogena wipes but stopped. She states that she has not used any lotions. She reports that she started using the metro cream after her last visit and has been using it after washing her face. She notes that she has not used the elidel cream.\n\nMEDICATIONS\n\n• Doxycycline 100mg PO BID\n\nREVIEW OF SYSTEMS\n\n• Skin: Denies any rash or itching.\n\nPHYSICAL EXAM\n\n• Skin: Fitzpatrick skin type IV. Bilateral medial cheeks show a few faint erythematous papules and mild redness around and underneath the nostrils.\n\nASSESSMENT AND PLAN\n\nMs. Miller has perioral dermatitis, rosacea, including ocular rosacea. She is advised to use the sulfacetamide wash twice a day and the metro cream as needed. She is instructed to apply the metro cream to the rash on her face twice daily until the rash resolves, then once a day for another week before stopping. She is advised to continue the doxycycline twice a day until the rash resolves and then once a day for another week before stopping. She is advised to use the vanicream as a moisturizer. She is advised to avoid using any anti-aging products or plant-based products.\n\nINSTRUCTIONS\n\n• Gently wash her eyelids once a day with a gentle cleanser such as vanicream. Use a Bruder mask as needed.\n• Use sulfacetamide wash twice a day.\n• Apply metro cream to the rash on her face twice daily until the rash resolves, then once a day for another week before stopping.\n• Continue doxycycline twice a day until the rash resolves and then once a day for another week before stopping.\n• Use vanicream as a moisturizer.\n• Avoid using any anti-aging products or plant-based products.\n\nMEDICATIONS\n\n• Doxycycline 100mg PO BID\n• Metro cream as needed\n• Sulfacetamide wash twice a day\n\nINSTRUCTIONS\n\n• Continue doxycycline until the rash resolves and then once a day for another week before stopping.\n• Use metro cream until the rash resolves.\n• Use sulfacetamide wash twice a day.\n• Use vanicream as a moisturizer.\n• Avoid using any anti-aging products or plant-based products.\n\nDISCLAIMER\n\nThis summary is a verbatim transcription of the conversation and is intended to be a medical record. It is not intended to be a substitute for a thorough medical evaluation and management. }}<|eot_id|>\n\n###Reference A