re has been running somewhat low, and he has been holding his Norvasc on occasion.\n- Patient Education and Counseling: I explained to the patient that we will hold the Norvasc until we get his pulmonary embolism ruled out.\n- Medical Treatment: Hold Norvasc.\n\nPatient Agreements: The patient understands and agrees with the recommended medical treatment plan. }}<|eot_id|>\n\n###Response B:\n{{ CHIEF COMPLAINT\n\nShortness of breath.\n\nHISTORY OF PRESENT ILLNESS\n\nDennis is a 57-year-old male who presents to the clinic today for the evaluation of shortness of breath. He reports that he has been feeling short of breath for the past couple of weeks. He states that he wakes up with shortness of breath and it persists even when he is at home. He reports that he has been coughing up blood for the past three weeks. He denies any chest pain, fever, chills, or recent travel. He has a history of diabetes and high blood pressure. He denies any leg swelling, bruising, or bleeding from his teeth or gums.\n\nThe patient states that he tried to get an appointment in the office but they were booked up. He denies any history of shortness of breath or difficulty breathing when he walks or exerts himself. He reports that his blood sugars have been running high and that he has been wearing a dexcom. He states that his blood sugars have been spiking up after meals and that his morning blood sugars are around 150. He denies any recent changes in his diet or exercise routine.\n\nThe patient states that his blood pressure has been running somewhat low and that he is taking norvasc. He reports that it has been running low on some days and has been as low as 90/60.\n\nREVIEW OF SYSTEMS\n\n• Constitutional: Reports of shortness of breath and coughing up blood.\n• Cardiovascular: Denies chest pain, fever, chills, or recent travel.\n• Gastrointestinal: Denies any nausea, vomiting, or abdominal pain.\n• Musculoskeletal: Denies any leg pain or swelling.\n• Respiratory: Reports of shortness of 