y. Tylenol can be used for severe pain. Prescription for Flexeril also provided.\n\n2. Diabetes.\n- Medical Reasoning: His diabetes is currently under control.\n- Patient Education and Counseling: We discussed the importance of maintaining a healthy lifestyle. I encouraged him to try an avoid foods high in sugar. He asked many questions and I feel he is confident in the plan at this point.\n- Medical Treatment: Hemoglobin A1c ordered as he has not had an updated one in almost 5 months. He will continue with his diet and exercise. Continue current dose of metformin for the time being. The patient will be checking his blood sugar in the morning and will log this information for me.\n\nPatient Agreements: The patient understands and agrees with the recommended medical treatment plan.\n\n#############################\n\n\n###Response A:\n{{ CHIEF COMPLAINT\n\nBack pain.\n\nHISTORY OF PRESENT ILLNESS\n\nNoah Johnson is a pleasant 50-year-old male who presents to the clinic today for the evaluation of back pain. The onset of his pain was approximately 1 week ago. He denies any specific injury. The patient states that he has tried over-the-counter medications with no relief.\n\nThe patient states that his pain is located in the mid-low back and radiates to the left side. The pain is aggravated by sudden movements. He denies any pain in his right leg or left leg.\n\nThe patient reports that he has a history of diabetes mellitus. He is currently on metformin 500 mg twice a day. He states that he tries to exercise when he can and occasionally tests his blood sugar in the morning.\n\nThe patient reports that he has been eating at the local bakery on Main Street. He states that he loves the bakery.\n\nREVIEW OF SYSTEMS\n\nConstitutional: Denies fever or weight loss.\nMusculoskeletal: Reports low back pain.\nNeurological: Denies headaches, dizziness, or numbness in the extremities.\n\nPHYSICAL EXAM\n\nNeurological: No focal deficits.\nRespiratory: Lungs are clear to auscultation bilaterally. No wheezes, rales, or rhonchi.\nCardiovascular: Regular rate and rhythm. No murmurs, gallops, or rubs.\nMusculoskeletal: Limited examination of the back due to patient\'s discomfort. No tenderness to palpation of the thoracic spine. Mild tenderness to palpation of the lumbar spine. Pain with extension and flexion of the lumbar spine. No pain with the straight leg raise bilaterally.\n\nRESULTS\n\nX-rays of the lumbar spine were obtained and reviewed today. These reveal no fractures, subluxations, or significant disc space narrowing.\n\nASSESSMENT AND PLAN\n\n1. Low back pain.\n• Medical Reasoning: The patient presents today with low back pain that began approximately 1 week ago. The pain is located in the mid-low back and radiates to the left side. The patient denies any specific injury. The pain is aggravated by sudden movements. The patient is currently on metformin for diabetes.\n• Additional Testing: X-rays of the lumbar spine were obtained and reviewed today. These reveal no fractures, subluxations, or significant disc space narrowing.\n• Medical Treatment: I recommended physical therapy for 2 reasons. The first reason is to help with pain. The second reason is to strengthen some of the muscles in the back. I also recommended that the patient take 400 mg of ibuprofen occasionally with tylenol as needed for pain, up to 3 times a day. I also prescribed flexeril 10 mg to be taken once a day every evening.\n• Patient Education and Counseling: I advised the patient to avoid strenuous or hazardous activities such as driving after taking muscle relaxants. I also advised the patient to watch his intake of sweets.\n\n2. Diabetes mellitus.\n• Medical Reasoning: The patient is currently on metformin 500 mg twice a day. He reports that he tries to exercise when he can and occasionally tests his blood sugar in the morning.\n• Additional Testing: I recommended that the patient obtain an HbA1c to check his blood sugar control. I advised him to check his blood pressure every morning.\n\nPatient Agreements: The patient understands and agrees with the recommended medical treatment plan.\n\nINSTRUCTIONS\n\nThe patient will follow up with me in 2 weeks. }}<|eot_id|>\n\n###Response B:\n{{ CHIEF COMPLAINT\n\nLow back pain.\n\nHISTORY OF PRESENT ILLNESS\n\nNoah is a 65-year-old male who presents to the clinic today for a follow-up visit for his diabetes. He states he has had low back pain for about a week. He denies any trauma or injury. He states he tried some over-the-counter medications but they did not help. He reports that he is having pain in the middle of his back that goes to the left side. He states that if he twists suddenly to the left, it gets worse. He denies any numbness or tingling in his feet. He reports that his diabetes has been stable. He denies any changes in his diet or exercise.\n\nREVIEW OF SYSTEMS\n\nMusculoskeletal: Reports low back pain.\n\nMEDICATIONS\n\nMetformin 500 mg twice daily.\n\nSOCIAL HISTORY\n\nPatient reports that he has a family history of diabetes. He denies smoking and drinking.\n\nVITALS\n\nPatient is afebrile.\n\nPHYSICAL EXAM\n\nNeurological: Muscle strength 5/5 in all extremities. No muscle atrophy. Reflexes 2+ bilaterally. No sensory deficits.\n\nMusculoskeletal: Positive pain to palpation in the lumbar region. No pain to palpation in the thoracic or cervical spine. No tenderness to palpation in the lower extremities.\n\nRESULTS\n\nX-ray of the lumbar spine ordered.\n\nASSESSMENT\n\nMusculoskeletal: Low back strain.\n\nPLAN\n\nI advised the patient to take 400 mg of ibuprofen as needed with Tylenol for pain relief. I prescribed Flexeril 10 mg once daily. I instructed him to avoid any strenuous or hazardous activities such as driving after taking the medication. I ordered an x-ray of the lumbar spine. I recommended that he continue with his current diet and exercise. I also recommended that he continue taking his metformin 500 mg twice daily. I advised him to take his blood glucose levels every morning and keep a record of them. I will see him back in 2 weeks for a follow-up.\n\nINSTRUCTIONS\n\nPatient was instructed to take 400 mg of ibuprofen as needed with Tylenol for pain relief. He was instructed to avoid any strenuous or hazardous activities such as driving after taking the medication. He was instructed to continue taking his metformin 500 mg twice daily. He was instructed to take his blood glucose levels every morning and keep a record of them. He was instructed to continue with his current diet and exercise. }}<|eot_id|>\n\n###Reference Answer:\nCHIEF COMPLAINT\n\nBack pain.\n\nMEDICAL HISTORY\n\nPatient reports history of diabetes.\n\nSOCIAL HISTORY\n\nThe patient\'s family owns and runs a bakery.\n\nMEDICATIONS\n\nPatient reports taking metformin twice daily.\n\nREVIEW OF SYSTEMS\n\nMusculoskeletal: Reports back pain.\nNeurological: Denies numbness or tingling in feet.\n\nPHYSICAL EXAM\n\nEyes\n- Examination: No redness, discharge, or icterus.\n\nEars, Nose, Mouth, and Throat\n- Examination of Ears: Auditory canal and tympanic membranes are clear.\n\nRespiratory\n- Auscultation of Lungs: Clear bilaterally.\n\nCardiovascular\n- Auscultation of Heart: Regular rate and rhythm.\nMusculoskeletal\n- Examination: No pain to palpation of the upper back. Pain to palpation of the midline of the lower back, especially on the left. No pain to palpation on the right lower back. Mildly positive straight leg rise on the right Positive straight leg rise on the left.\n\nASSESSMENT AND PLAN\n\n1. Low back pain.\n- Medical Reasoning: I believe he has a musculoskeletal low back strain. I do not expect to see anything significant on x-ray, but we will obtain one for further evaluation of any possible disc issues.\n- Patient Education and Counseling: We discussed his diagnosis at length today. I explained that physical therapy can help with the pain, but also strengthen some of the muscles in the back. I explained that he needs to be careful while taking medic