 physical therapy appointments. }}<|eot_id|>\n\n###Response B:\n{{ CHIEF COMPLAINT\n\nBack pain.\n\nHISTORY OF PRESENT ILLNESS\n\nDennis is a 35-year-old male who presents to the clinic today for evaluation of right heel pain. He reports that the pain is located in the back of his heel and radiates up into his calf. He states that the pain is present after activity and when he first wakes up in the morning. He states that the pain is relieved with activity and then returns after sitting for a long period of time. He denies any trauma to the area.\n\nThe patient reports that he is currently training for a triathlon and has been doing this for about 6 months. He states that he runs about 10 km in the morning, bikes for about 45 minutes to an hour at lunch, and swims for about 30 minutes to an hour in the evening. He reports that he is doing this 5 days a week and takes one day off. He states that he does not stretch before or after his workouts.\n\nThe patient denies any numbness or tingling in his legs. He reports that he is able to run and bike without any issues.\n\nVITALS\n\nThe patient\'s vital signs were normal.\n\nPHYSICAL EXAM\n\nNeurovascular examination of the lower extremities is grossly normal.\n\nMusculoskeletal examination of the lower extremities is significant for pain to palpation of the right Achilles tendon. The patient reports pain to palpation of the right Achilles tendon. The patient reports pain to palpation of the right Achilles tendon. He reports pain to palpation of the right Achilles tendon. The patient reports pain to palpation of the right Achilles tendon. The patient reports pain to palpation of the right Achilles tendon.\n\nASSESSMENT AND PLAN\n\nAchilles tendinitis.\n\nThe patient is a 35-year-old male who presents to the clinic today for evaluation of right heel pain. He reports that the pain is located in the back of his heel and radiates up into his calf. He states that the pain is present after activity and when he first wakes up in