vascular: Denies chest pain. Endorses lower extremity edema.\nRespiratory: Denies shortness of breath.\n\nVITALS\n\nVital signs are within normal limits.\n\nPHYSICAL EXAM\n\nNeck: No jugular venous distention. No carotid bruits.\nCardiovascular: Grade 3/6 systolic ejection murmur heard at the left base.\nRespiratory: Clear to auscultation bilaterally.\nExtremities: 1+ pitting edema in the lower extremities.\nMusculoskeletal: No focal tenderness. Radial artery cath site is clean, dry, and intact without hematoma. Right radial artery pulses are palpable.\n\nRESULTS\n\nEKG: Normal sinus rhythm, R wave progression, and evolutionary changes consistent with a STEMI.\n\nASSESSMENT AND PLAN\n\n1. Coronary artery disease.\n- Medical Reasoning: The patient had a recent interior STEMI and was placed on a stent. She is currently compliant with her medications.\n- Patient Education and Counseling: We discussed the importance of continuing her medications, especially aspirin and brilinta, and maintaining a healthy diet. She was advised to continue her statin, toprol, and lisinopril.\n- Medical Treatment: Continue aspirin 81 mg daily, brilinta 90 mg twice daily, lipitor 80 mg daily, and toprol 50 mg daily.\n\n2. Newly reduced left ventricular dysfunction and moderate mitral regurgitation.\n- Medical Reasoning: The patient had a recent echocardiogram showing an ejection fraction of 35%.\n- Patient Education and Counseling: We discussed the findings of her echocardiogram and the importance of continuing her medications. We also discussed cardiac rehab as an option.\n- Medical Treatment: Referral to cardiac rehab. Continue lisinopril 20 mg daily.\n\n3. Acute heart failure.\n- Medical Reasoning: The patient has been retaining fluid.\n- Patient Education and Counseling: We discussed the importance of dietary modifications and monitoring her weight. We also discussed the use of diuretics.\n- Medical Treatment: Prescribed Lasix 40 mg once a day. Add Aldactone 12.5 mg daily.\n\n4. Hypertension.\n- Medical Reasoning: The patient\'s blood pressure seems fine.\n- Patient Education and Counseling: We discussed the importance of dietary modifications and monitoring her blood pressure.\n- Medical Treatment: Continue Aldactone 12.5 mg daily.\n\nINSTRUCTIONS\n\nThe patient will follow up in 2 months for repeat echocardiogram. }}<|eot_id|>\n\n###Response B:\n{{ CHIEF COMPLAINT\n\nAcute chest pain.\n\nHISTORY OF PRESENT ILLNESS\n\nJulia is a 65-year-old female who presents to the clinic for a follow-up after an interior ST elevation myocardial infarction (STEMI) where she had another stent placed. She reports that she had been doing well since her last heart attack but recently started having chest pain that would not go away. She reports that it is similar to what she experienced last time. She denies chest pain or shortness of breath since her last visit.\n\nShe states that she has been walking her dog every day and going about a mile and a half to two miles. She is excited for fall and has no plans for the holiday.\n\nShe reports that she has a history of coronary artery disease, hypertension, and diabetes. She has noticed some swelling in her legs since being discharged from the hospital. She is able to lie flat at night but has had to sit up a little bit more. She is able to afford her medications and is taking them as prescribed. She has not been watching her salt intake since her last heart attack. She loves pizza and tries are her second favorite food. She is taking metformin and trying to stay active.\n\nSOCIAL HISTORY\n\nShe is married and enjoys walking her dog.\n\nREVIEW OF SYSTEMS\n\n• Constitutional: Denies fever, chills, or fatigue.\n• Cardiovascular: Denies chest pain, shortness of breath, or palpitations.\n• Musculoskeletal: Denies muscle weakness or joint pain.\n• Respiratory: Denies shortness of breath or cough.\n• Gastrointestinal: Denies nausea, vomiting, or abdominal pain.\n• Neurological: Denies headache, seizure, or stroke.\n\nPHYSICAL EXAM\n\nNeck Exam: No jugular venous distension (JVD) appreciated. No carotid bruits appreciated.\nRespiratory: Lungs are clear to auscultation bilaterally.\nCardiovascular: Regular rate and rhythm. Systolic ejection murmur 3/6 heard at the left base. No S3 or S4 gallop. No jugular venous distension (JVD) appreciated. No carotid bruits appreciated.\nExtremities: 1+ pitting edema on the right lower extremity. No skin changes or erythema. No clubbing or edema. No radial artery pulses appreciated.\nLower Extremities: 1+ pitting edema on the right lower extremity. No skin changes or erythema. No clubbing or edema. No pedal edema.\n\nRESULTS\n\nEKG: Normal sinus rhythm with a good R wave progression. Evolutionary changes consistent with a recent STEMI.\n\nASSESSMENT AND PLAN\n\n• Coronary Artery Disease: Medical Reasoning: The patient has a history of coronary artery disease. Given her recent STEMI and reduced ejection fraction of 35%, she will continue to be monitored. Medical Reasoning: She will continue her aspirin 81 mg daily, brilinta 90 mg twice daily, lipitor 80 mg daily, and toprol 50 mg daily. She will be referred to cardiac rehabilitation for education and exercise.\n\n• Reduced Left Ventricular Function and Moderate Mitral Regurgitation: Medical Reasoning: The patient has reduced left ventricular function and moderate mitral regurgitation. Given her history of coronary artery disease, this is consistent with a stunned myocardium. Medical Reasoning: She will continue her lisinopril 20 mg daily.\n\n• Acute Heart Failure: Medical Reasoning: The patient has acute heart failure with pitting edema 1+ on her right lower extremity. Medical Reasoning: She will be started on lasix 40 mg daily and aldactone 12.5 mg daily.\n\n• Hypertension: Medical Reasoning: The patient has hypertension. Medical Reasoning: She will be referred to nutrition counseling for dietary modifications. }}<|eot_id|>\n\n###Reference Answer:\nCHIEF COMPLAINT\n\nFollow up status post inferior STEMI