.\n\n4. Depression.\n- Referral to Dr. John Smith.\n\nPatient Agreements: The patient understands and agrees with the recommended medical treatment plan. }}<|eot_id|>\n\n###Response B:\n{{ CHIEF COMPLAINT\n\nPatient reports having a heart attack about 6 months ago and is concerned about the medications he is taking.\n\nMEDICAL HISTORY\n\nPatient reports a history of coronary artery disease, for which he had a heart attack 6 months ago. He had a stent placed in his left anterior descending artery. He also reports a history of diabetes. He reports that he had an a1c of 60 and a glucose of 130. He reports that he is not taking any medication for his diabetes.\n\nSOCIAL HISTORY\n\nPatient reports that he is a retired teacher. He reports that he lives with his dog and is a fan of the San Francisco 49ers.\n\nPHYSICAL EXAM\n\nExamination of the neck is unremarkable. Examination of the heart reveals a 3/6 systolic ejection murmur at the left base. Examination of the lower extremities reveals 1+ non-pitting edema.\n\nRESULTS\n\nPatient had a kidney function test which was stable. He has stage 3 chronic kidney disease. His kidney function was normal.\n\nASSESSMENT AND PLAN\n\nPatient reports having a heart attack about 6 months ago and is concerned about the medications he is taking. The patient is on a fair amount of medications. He is taking aspirin, brilinta, and crestor. He is concerned that the crestor may be causing muscle aches. He reports that he gets dizzy when he stands up quickly. He is taking metoprolol 50mg daily. The patient asks how long he will have to take the stent. The doctor explains that the stent is permanent and that he will have to take aspirin for the rest of his life and brilinta for another 6 months. He is concerned about replacing the stent. The doctor explains that he will not have to replace the stent and that he will have to follow up with the cardiologist. The patient is concerned about his diabetes. He reports that he had an a1c of 60 and a glucose of 130. He reports that he is not taking any medication for his diabetes. The patient reports that his a1c was not where he wanted it to be but his glucose is coming in at 1:30. The doctor orders a referral for diabetic education. The patient reports that he had a blood test. He reports that his kidney function was stable. The doctor notes that his kidney function was stable. The patient reports that he has stage 3 chronic kidney disease. He reports that he is not taking any pain medication. The doctor recommends that he take tylenol. The patient reports that he has some puffiness in his legs. The doctor notes that this is due to the medications. The patient reports that he is a fan of the San Francisco 49ers. The doctor asks if he is excited about the super bowl. The patient reports that he is a fan of Jimmy G. The doctor recommends that he talk to his nephrologist. The patient reports that he has not seen his nephrologist in a year. The doctor recommends that he follow up with his nephrologist. The patient reports that he is not doing well since he had his heart attack. The doctor asks if he is having any suicidal thoughts. The patient reports that he is just feeling down. The doctor notes that he is not having any suicidal thoughts. The patient reports that he lives with his dog. The doctor recommends that he talk to a psychiatrist. The patient agrees to talk to a psychiatrist. }}<|eot_id|>\n\n###Reference Answer:\nCHIEF COMPLAINT\n\nFollow-up.\n\nMEDICAL HISTORY\n\nPatient reports having a heart attack 6 months ago, coronary artery disease, diabetes.\n\nSURGICAL HISTORY\n\nPatient reports history of stent placement at the left anterior descending artery.\n\nSOCIAL HISTORY\n\nPatient reports that he is from the west coast and is a San Francisco 49ers fan. He lives with his dog.\n\nMEDICATIONS\n\nPatient reports taking aspirin, Brilinta, fish oil, Crestor, metoprolol 50 mg once a day,\n\nREVIEW OF SYSTEMS\n\nMusculoskeletal: Reports non-pitting lower extremi