d to the left. Positive Tinel\'s sign.\n\nASSESSMENT\n\nRight carpal tunnel syndrome.\n\nPLAN\n\nAfter reviewing her symptoms and exam findings, I believe her symptoms are secondary to carpal tunnel syndrome. We discussed the nature of this condition, as well as her treatment options, in detail. I recommend we treat this conservatively with nighttime wrist splinting and ibuprofen, but I want to increase her dose to 600 mg 4 times daily. I\'m also going to order an EMG/Nerve conduction study for further evaluation. If this is positive, we can consider additional treatment with physical therapy or surgical intervention with carpal tunnel release.\n#############################\n\n\n###Response A:\n{{ CHIEF COMPLAINT\n\nRight hand numbness and tingling.\n\nHISTORY OF PRESENT ILLNESS\n\nMs. Alexis Williams is a pleasant 34-year-old female who presents to the clinic today for evaluation of right hand numbness and tingling. The onset of her symptoms is unclear, but she reports that over the past month, she has experienced intermittent numbness and tingling in her right hand. She denies any significant pain, but she states that the pain is worse when she is typing on the keyboard. She has been using ibuprofen for pain relief. The patient states that not typing on the keyboard and not using the mouse alleviates her pain. She denies that her symptoms have affected her sleep.\n\nREVIEW OF SYSTEMS\n\nMusculoskeletal: Reports right hand pain, numbness, and tingling. Denies left hand pain.\nNeurological: Denies numbness or tingling in the left hand.\n\nPHYSICAL EXAM\n\nMSK: Examination of the right hand reveals pain with wrist extension and flexion. Decreased sensation in the right thumb, index, and middle fingers. Positive Tinel\'s sign.\n\nRESULTS\n\nWe have ordered EMG and Nerve Conduction Study of the right upper extremity. We are awaiting the results.\n\nASSESSMENT\n\n1. Carpal Tunnel Syndrome, Right Upper Extremity.\n2. Hypothyroidism.\n\nPLAN\n\nAfter reviewing the patien