 and my nurse will be in with those prescriptions\n[patient] alright thank you\n#############################\n\nHere is the ground truth note:\n#############################\nCHIEF COMPLAINT\n\nRight elbow pain.\n\nREVIEW OF SYSTEMS\n\nCardiovascular: Denies chest pain.\nGastrointestinal: Denies abdominal pain.\nMusculoskeletal: Reports right elbow pain.\n\nVITALS\n\nBlood pressure: 150/90 mmHg\n\nPHYSICAL EXAM\n\nRespiratory\n- Auscultation of Lungs: Clear bilaterally.\n\nCardiovascular\nGrade 2/6 systolic ejection murmur.\n\nMusculoskeletal\n- Examination: Pain to palpation of the olecranon area of the posterior elbow. Mild pain with flexion and extension but otherwise normal range of motion. There is swelling about the elbow, as well as mild ecchymosis.\n\nHematology/Lymphatic/Immunology\n- Examination: Bruising noted over the right elbow.\n\nRESULTS\n\nX-ray of the right elbow was reviewed and demonstrates no evidence of fracture or bony abnormalities.\n\nASSESSMENT AND PLAN\n\n1. Right elbow contusion.\n- Medical Reasoning: The patient sustained an injury to his elbow when his son struck him with a lacrosse stick. His x-ray and exam findings are consistent with an elbow contusion.\n- Patient Education and Counseling: I encouraged the patient to avoid playing lacrosse for the next couple of weeks, just until his elbow heals. After that, he should be fine to continue playing normally.\n- Medical Treatment: I\'m going to prescribe him ibuprofen 800 mg twice daily to help with some of the pain and swelling. He should also ice the area in 20-minute intervals 3 times per day to further reduce his symptoms.\n\n2. Hypertension.\n- Medical Reasoning: This appears to be well-controlled with dietary modifications and lisinopril.\n- Patient Education and Counseling: I encouraged him to keep up with his dietary modifications.\n- Medical Treatment: We will keep him on lisinopril 20 mg daily.\n\n3. Diabetes.\n- Medical Reasoning: This appears to be well controlled at this time.\n- Medical Treatment: We will keep him on metformin 500 mg and order a hemoglobin A1c to evaluate any need for medication adjustments.\n\nPatient Agreements: The patient understands and agrees with the recommended medical treatment plan.\n#############################\n\n\n###Response A:\n{{ CHIEF COMPLAINT\n\nRight elbow pain.\n\nHISTORY OF PRESENT ILLNESS\n\nPhilip Harris is a pleasant 65-year-old male who presents to the clinic today for the evaluation of right elbow pain. The onset of his pain began one week ago while playing a game of lacrosse with his children. He denies any specific fall but notes that his child was a bit rough with him. He describes the pain as sharp and localized to the lateral aspect of his elbow. The pain radiates down his forearm. He has tried taking ibuprofen with some relief.\n\nREVIEW OF SYSTEMS\n\nMusculoskeletal: Reports right elbow pain.\n\nVITALS\n\nBlood pressure: 150/90.\n\nPHYSICAL EXAM\n\nRespiratory: Lungs are clear to auscultation bilaterally.\nCardiovascular: Grade 2/6 systolic ejection murmur.\nMusculoskeletal: Right elbow exam: Pain to palpation of the olecranon area of the posterior elbow. Mild pain with flexion and extension. Normal range of motion.\n\nRESULTS\n\nX-ray of the right elbow: Normal, no fracture or bony abnormalities.\n\nASSESSMENT\n\n1. Right elbow contusion.\n2. Hypertension.\n3. Diabetes mellitus.\n\nPLAN\n\n1. Right elbow contusion:\n- Medical Reasoning: Patient has right elbow pain after playing lacrosse with his son. He is having difficulty with activities that require him to use his right arm. X-ray of the right elbow was normal.\n- Patient Education and Counseling: I advised the patient to ice his elbow three times a day for 20 minutes at a time and take ibuprofen 800 mg twice a day as needed for pain.\n- Medical Treatment: Prescribed ibuprofen 800 mg, oral, twice a day as needed for pain.\n\n2. Hypertension:\n- Medical Reasoning: Patient\'s blood pressure is elevated today. He is currently on 20 mg of lisinopril daily.\n- Patient Education and Counseling: Advised the patient to continue taking his lisinopril daily and to continue his low salt diet.\n- Medical Treatment: Continue lisinopril 20 mg daily.\n\n3. Diabetes mellitus:\n- Medical Reasoning: Patient is currently on 500 mg of metformin daily. He reports his blood sugars are between 80 to 100.\n- Patient Education and Counseling: Advised the patient to continue taking his metformin daily. We will obtain a hemoglobin A1c to assess his current blood sugar levels.\n- Medical Treatment: Continue metformin 500 mg daily. Obtain hemoglobin A1c.\n\nPatient Agreements: The patient understands and agrees with the recommended medical treatment plan. }}<|eot_id|>\n\n###Response B:\n{{ CHIEF COMPLAINT\n\nRight elbow pain.\n\nHISTORY OF PRESENT ILLNESS\n\nPhilip is a 63-year-old male who presents to the clinic today for evaluation of his right elbow pain. He reports that he hurt his right elbow about a week ago while playing lacrosse with his kids. He denies any recent trauma or falls. He states that his oldest child is 13 years old and the other is 10 years old. He was playing with a 13-year-old. He denies any recent trauma or falls.\n\nHe reports that his elbow hurts at the tip of the elbow and hurts with any movement. He denies any numbness or tingling in his arm. He states that he has tried taking Advil for the pain, but it has not helped much. He reports that the pain radiates down his forearm.\n\nThe patient reports a past medical history of hypertension and diabetes.\n\nThe patient reports that he is currently taking 20 mg of lisinopril daily for his hypertension. He states that he is compliant with his medication and that he has been following a Mediterranean diet. He reports that he does not add salt to his food and that the whole family follows a low-sodium diet.\n\nThe patient reports that he is taking 500 mg of metformin daily for his diabetes. He states that he does not check his blood sugar levels every day, but when he does, his levels are usually between 80 and 100. He denies any chest pain or shortness of breath.\n\nREVIEW OF SYSTEMS\n\n• Constitutional: Reports that he has been doing well.\n• Musculoskeletal: Reports pain in his right elbow.\n• Neurological: Denies any numbness or tingling in his arm.\n\nPHYSICAL EXAMINATION\n\n• Respiratory: Lungs are clear to auscultation bilaterally.\n• Cardiovascular: Grade 2 out of 6 systolic ejection murmur.\n• Musculoskeletal: Right elbow: Examination of the right elbow shows pain to palpation of the olecranon area of the posterior elbow. Mild pain with flexion and extension of the right elbow. Normal range of motion of the right elbow.\n\nRESULTS\n\n• Right elbow x-ray: Negative for fracture or bony abnormality.\n\nASSESSMENT AND PLAN\n\n• Right elbow contusion. The patient reports pain in his right elbow after playing lacrosse with his kids. Examination of the right elbow shows pain to palpation of the olecranon area of the posterior elbow. Mild pain with flexion and extension of the right elbow. Normal range of motion of the right elbow. The patient is prescribed 800 mg of ibuprofen twice a day for pain and swelling. He is instructed to ice the area three times a day for 20 minutes at a time. The patient is advised to avoid playing lacrosse for the next couple of weeks to allow the area to heal.\n• Hypertension: The patient is compliant with his 20 mg of lisinopril daily. He is advised to continue this medication.\n• Diabetes: The patient is compliant with his 500 mg of metformin daily. He is advised to continue this medication. We will order an A1c to check his levels and adjust his medication as needed. }}<|eot_id|>\n\n###Reference Answer:\nCHIEF COMPLAINT\n\nRight elbow pain.\n\nREVIEW OF SYSTEMS\n\nCardiovascular: Denies chest pain.\nGastrointestinal: Denies abdominal pain.\nMusculoskeletal: Reports right elbow pain.\n\nVITALS\n\nBlood pressure: 150/90 mmHg\n\nPHYSICAL EXAM\n\nRespiratory\n- Auscultation of Lu