ss of breath, chest pain, or difficulty sleeping. He states he has been getting up and moving around without any problems. He has been monitoring his blood pressure at home and it has been good. The patient has been compliant with his medications since discharge.\n\nREVIEW OF SYSTEMS\n\nConstitutional: Denies fever or chills. Endocrine: Denies polyuria. Cardiovascular: Denies chest pain. Respiratory: Denies shortness of breath. Gastrointestinal: Denies nausea or vomiting. Genitourinary: Denies dysuria. Musculoskeletal: Denies muscle pain. Neurological: Denies dizziness.\n\nVITALS\n\nBlood pressure: 128/72. Heart rate: 79. Respiratory rate: 26. O2 sat: 99%.\n\nPHYSICAL EXAM\n\nNeck: No jugular venous distention.\nCardiovascular: 2/6 systolic ejection murmur, stable.\nRespiratory: Clear to auscultation bilaterally.\nExtremities: Trace edema in the lower extremities.\n\nRESULTS\n\nEchocardiogram shows a preserved EF of 55%, abnormal diastolic filling, and mild to moderate mitral regurgitation.\n\nASSESSMENT AND PLAN\n\n1. Acute on chronic heart failure.\n- Medical Reasoning: The patient\'s symptoms of ankle swelling and dyspnea on exertion are consistent with CHF. His echocardiogram shows a preserved EF with abnormal diastolic filling and mild to moderate mitral regurgitation.\n- Medical Treatment: Continue Bumex 2 mg daily. Continue to monitor diet and avoid salty foods. Weigh daily and call if he gains 3 pounds in 2 days. Referral to a nutritionist for education on low-sodium diet.\n\n2. Hypertension.\n- Medical Reasoning: The patient\'s blood pressure was elevated at 200/90 during his admission.\n- Medical Treatment: Continue Cozaar 100 mg daily. Continue Norvasc 5 mg daily. Renal artery ultrasound ordered to rule out secondary causes.\n\n3. Stage 3 chronic kidney disease.\n- Medical Reasoning: The patient has a known history of stage 3 CKD.\n- Medical Treatment: Blood work ordered to assess renal function.\n\nPatient Agreements: The patient understands and agrees with the recommended medical treatment plan. }}<|eot_id|>\n\n###Response B:\n{{ CHIEF COMPLAINT\n\nAcute on chronic CHF exacerbation.\n\nHISTORY OF PRESENT ILLNESS\n\nKevin is an 82-year-old male who presents for hospital follow-up after an acute on chronic CHF exacerbation. He reports that he was doing well until he noticed his ankles were swelling. He denies chest pain, shortness of breath, or difficulty sleeping. He reports that he had a high blood pressure reading in the ER. He states that he takes his medications most of the time, but sometimes misses a dose or two. He admits to watching his diet, but notes that he enjoys watching football and loves pizza and chicken wings. He reports that he was in the hospital for 2 days and was given medication that made him urinate frequently and then had an IV of water and diuretic. He reports that he has been doing better since his hospitalization and has been taking his medications as prescribed.\n\nVITALS\n\nVital signs are stable with a blood pressure of 128/72 mmHg.\n\nPHYSICAL EXAM\n\nNeck Exam: No jugular venous distension.\n\nHeart Exam: 2/6 systolic ejection murmur, stable.\n\nLung Exam: Clear to auscultation bilaterally.\n\nExtremities Exam: 1+ pitting edema in bilateral lower extremities.\n\nRESULTS\n\nEchocardiogram: Preserved ejection fraction of 55% with abnormal diastolic filling and mild to moderate mitral regurgitation.\n\nASSESSMENT AND PLAN\n\nMr. Kevin presents with congestive heart failure, hypertension, and stage 3 chronic kidney disease.\n\nPatient\'s congestive heart failure is secondary to dietary indiscretion and uncontrolled hypertension. He will continue on bumex 2 mg daily. He is advised to continue watching his diet and avoid salty foods. He is to weigh himself daily and call if he gains 3 pounds in 2 days.\n\nPatient\'s hypertension will continue on cozaar 100 mg daily and norvasc 5 mg once daily.\n\nPatient\'s stage 3 chronic kidney disease will require further evaluation with a renal ultraso