nt regimen.\n- Patient Education and Counseling: I encouraged the patient to keep her appointment with ophthalmology for evaluation of diabetic retinopathy.\n- Medical Treatment: I\'m going to put in an order for a repeat hemoglobin A1c to check for any needed medication adjustments and have her follow up in 2 weeks. In the meantime, I want her to continue with metformin 500 mg twice daily and dietary modifications.\n\n3. Hypertension.\n- Medical Reasoning: Several providers, including myself, have noted a trend of elevated blood pressures in the patient\'s chart.\n- Patient Education and Counseling: I advised the patient to monitor her blood pressures at home over the next 2 weeks and report her readings to me via MyChart. This should help us determine if any adjustments need to be made to her new medications. We discussed the possible side effects of hydrochlorothiazide how this will hopefully reduce some of her swelling seen on physical exam. All of her questions were answered.\n- Medical Treatment: I\'m going to have her start hydrochlorothiazide at a low dose.\n\nPatient Agreements: The patient understands and agrees with the recommended medical treatment plan.\n\nINSTRUCTIONS\n\nThe patient will follow up in 2 weeks.\n#############################\n\n\n###Response A:\n{{ CHIEF COMPLAINT\n\nChest pain.\n\nMEDICAL HISTORY\n\nPatient reports history of diabetes and hypertension.\n\nSURGICAL HISTORY\n\nPatient reports history of diabetes and hypertension.\n\nREVIEW OF SYSTEMS\n\nCardiovascular: Reports chest pain.\nRespiratory: Denies shortness of breath.\nGastrointestinal: Denies nausea, vomiting, diarrhea.\nMusculoskeletal: Reports swelling in the legs.\n\nVITALS\n\nBlood pressure: Elevated in clinic today.\n\nPHYSICAL EXAM\n\nNeck\n- General Examination: No JVD.\n- Carotid: No bruits.\n\nRespiratory\n- Auscultation of Lungs: Clear bilaterally. No wheezes, rales, or rhonchi.\n\nCardiovascular\n- Auscultation of Heart: Regular rate and rhythm. 2/6 systolic ejection murmur.\n- Palpation: Pulses are equal.\n\nGastrointestinal\n- Examination: Soft, non-tender, no masses.\n\nMusculoskeletal\n- Examination: 1+ non-pitting edema in bilateral lower extremities.\n\nRESULTS\n\nEKG reviewed and is normal.\n\nASSESSMENT AND PLAN\n\n1. Chest pain.\n- Medical Reasoning: The patient presents with chest pain that occurs with exertion. Considering her history of diabetes and hypertension, as well as her family history of heart disease, I am concerned for unstable angina. Her EKG today is normal, so no acute myocardial infarction.\n- Patient Education and Counseling: I explained the risks and benefits of starting aspirin therapy. I also explained the use of nitroglycerin.\n- Medical Treatment: Prescribed aspirin 81 mg daily. Prescribed nitroglycerin 0.4 mg to be taken sublingually as needed for chest pain.\n\n2. Diabetes.\n- Medical Reasoning: The patient is doing fairly well with her diabetes. We will continue to monitor her A1c and will have her follow up in 2 weeks.\n- Patient Education and Counseling: I encouraged the patient to continue her diet and exercise regimen. I advised her to avoid soda.\n- Medical Treatment: Continue metformin 500 mg twice daily.\n\n3. Hypertension.\n- Medical Reasoning: The patient has had elevated blood pressure readings in clinic today, as well as from several other offices. Considering her history and the chest pain, I am concerned for secondary hypertension.\n- Patient Education and Counseling: I explained the risks and benefits of starting hydrochlorothiazide. I advised her to monitor her blood pressure at home.\n- Medical Treatment: Prescribed hydrochlorothiazide 25 mg daily.\n\nPatient Agreements: The patient understands and agrees with the recommended medical treatment plan. }}<|eot_id|>\n\n###Response B:\n{{ CHIEF COMPLAINT\n\nChest Pain\n\nHISTORY OF PRESENT ILLNESS\n\nElizabeth is a 53-year-old female who presents to the office today for evaluation of chest pain. She reports that she has had chest 