ets every 5 minutes for 3 doses if her chest pain does not resolve. She should then go to the emergency room if her pain does not resolve.\n• Diabetes: The patient reports that she is having difficulty with her blood sugars. She reports that she has been exercising but has been having trouble with her blood sugars. She denies nausea, vomiting, or diarrhea. She is currently taking metformin 500 mg twice a day. She reports that she has had an appointment with ophthalmology in 2 weeks. I am going to continue her metformin 500 mg twice a day. I am going to order another hemoglobin A1c today. I am going to have her return in 2 weeks and we will review her results.\n• Hypertension: The patient reports that she has been having high blood pressure. She denies nausea, vomiting, or diarrhea. I am going to start her on hydrochlorothiazide. I am going to have her use a blood pressure cuff at home and enter her results into my chart. I will review her results at her next visit.\n\nINSTRUCTIONS\n\nThe patient should take her medications as directed. She should return in 2 weeks for a follow-up appointment. She should also return to her exercise routine once her chest pain has resolved. }}<|eot_id|>\n\n###Reference Answer:\nCHIEF COMPLAINT\n\nChest pain on exertion.\n\nMEDICAL HISTORY\n\nThe patient has a medical history of diabetes type 2. She has not been formally diagnosed with hypertension, but her blood pressures have been trending high.\n\nFAMILY HISTORY\n\nPatient reports family history of heart disease.\n\nMEDICATIONS\n\nPatient reports taking metformin 500 mg twice daily.\n\nREVIEW OF SYSTEMS\n\nConstitutional: Denies diaphoresis or clamminess.\nCardiovascular: Reports chest pain.\nRespiratory: Denies dyspnea.\nGastrointestinal: Denies nausea, vomiting, or diarrhea.\n\nPHYSICAL EXAM\n\nNeck\n- General Examination: No JVD, swelling, or carotid bruits.\n\nRespiratory\n- Assessment of Respiratory Effort:\n- Auscultation of Lungs: Clear bilaterally. No wheezes, rales, or rhon