gthy discussion with the patient in regard to his current symptoms. I have explained that his symptoms are in line with a herniated disc at L4-L5 with nerve impingement. I recommended a spinal injection with a strong NSAID and a corticosteroid, which I will inject into the lumbar spine under fluoroscopy. The patient is in agreement with this plan. All questions were answered to the patient\'s satisfaction.\n\nINSTRUCTIONS\n\nThe patient will follow up with me in 1 week for reassessment. }}<|eot_id|>\n\n###Response B:\n{{ CHIEF COMPLAINT\n\nBack pain.\n\nHISTORY OF PRESENT ILLNESS\n\nKyle is a 25-year-old male who presents to the clinic today for evaluation of his low back pain. He states that he has been experiencing this pain for approximately 2 weeks. He reports that he was lifting a box of books and felt a pop in his back. He denies any previous injury. He denies any recent trauma.\n\nHe states that the pain is achy and sharp in his low back. He reports that the pain radiates down his left leg. He denies any numbness, tingling, or loss of sensation. He denies any loss of bladder or bowel control.\n\nHe reports that he has been taking ibuprofen and Tylenol for his pain. He reports that this has been somewhat helpful. He denies any history of back surgery or any family history of spine conditions. He denies any smoking.\n\nHe reports that he plays soccer and used to play midfield. He reports that he is a fan of the Tatuham Hotsper. He reports that he is currently sedentary.\n\nPHYSICAL EXAM\n\nVital signs: Blood pressure is 130/70 mmHg. Respiratory rate is 18 breaths per minute. Heart rate is regular and regular. No murmurs, rubs, or gallops appreciated on cardiac exam. Lungs are clear to auscultation bilaterally.\n\nMusculoskeletal exam: Back exam: The patient has no overlying redness or bruising. He has midline tenderness at the L4-L5 disk space. He has right-sided lumbar paravertebral tenderness. He is able to bend forward without pain. He is able to bend backwa