emporary relief, as well as wrapping her knee with kinesiology tape. She states that she is unable to take ibuprofen secondary to an ulcer.\n\nPHYSICAL EXAM\n\nMSK: Examination of the left knee: Edema and effusion noted. Moderate ROM. Pain with flexion and extension of the knee. Negative Lachman\'s. Negative valgus and varus stress test.\n\nRESULTS\n\n4 views of the left knee were taken. These reveal no evidence of any fractures or dislocations. No other abnormalities are noted.\n\nASSESSMENT\n\nLeft knee pain, possible MCL strain.\n\nPLAN\n\nAfter reviewing the patient\'s examination and radiographic findings today, I have discussed with the patient that her x-rays did not reveal any signs of a tear. I have recommended that we treat the patient conservatively. The plan is to place the patient in a knee immobilizer to provide increased support for the next week. I have also prescribed the patient tramadol 50 mg to treat her pain. She can take Tylenol as needed for breakthrough pain. I have also advised her to ice her left knee 3 to 4 times a day for 20 to 30 minutes at a time. I have advised her to refrain from running for the next 3 weeks. If her pain does not improve with the immobilizer, I will recommend obtaining an MRI.\n\n#############################\n\n\n###Response A:\n{{ CHIEF COMPLAINT\n\nLeft knee pain.\n\nHISTORY OF PRESENT ILLNESS\n\nAngela Johnson is a pleasant 29-year-old female who presents to the clinic today for the evaluation of left knee pain. The onset of her pain began 1 week ago when she was running around with her 5-year-old niece. She states she felt a pop in her knee at the time of injury. The patient denies any previous trauma to the knee. She states her pain is located on the medial and lateral aspect of her knee. The patient states she has been limping since the injury. The patient states she has been taking Tylenol for pain and wrapping her knee with kinesiology tape. She denies taking ibuprofen as she has an ulcer. She states the Tylenol helps a little bit. The patient states she is unable to bear weight on her left leg.\n\nREVIEW OF SYSTEMS\n\nMusculoskeletal: Reports left knee pain.\n\nPHYSICAL EXAM\n\nExamination of the left knee: There is edema present. There is effusion. There is moderate range of motion. There is pain with flexion and extension of the knee. Negative Lachman\'s test. Negative varus and valgus stress test.\n\nRESULTS\n\nX-rays of the left knee were obtained and reviewed today. There are no fractures or bony abnormalities.\n\nASSESSMENT\n\nLeft knee pain.\n\nPLAN\n\nAfter reviewing the patient\'s examination and radiographic findings today, I have had a lengthy discussion with the patient in regards to her current symptoms. I have explained to the patient the nature of her diagnosis. I have recommended that we treat the patient conservatively. I have recommended that we start the patient on a pain medication that is not an NSAID. I have prescribed the patient Tramadol 50 mg, oral, twice a day, as needed for pain. She can also take Tylenol for breakthrough pain. I have recommended that we place the patient in a knee immobilizer for the next week to keep the knee from moving and to reduce some of the inflammation that she is seeing. I have recommended that she ice her knee 3 to 4 times a day for 20 to 30 minutes at a time. I have also advised her that she should not run for the next 3 weeks.\n\nINSTRUCTIONS\n\nThe patient will follow up with me in 2 weeks if she is not improving. }}<|eot_id|>\n\n###Response B:\n{{ CHIEF COMPLAINT\n\nLeft knee pain.\n\nHISTORY OF PRESENT ILLNESS\n\nAngela is a 34-year-old female who presents today for evaluation of left knee pain. She states she hurt her knee about a week ago when she was running around with her 5-year-old niece who is learning to ride a bike without training wheels. She states she fell and twisted her knee in a funky way. She denies hearing a pop. She reports that her pain is on the inside of her knee, more on the medial side than the lateral side. She states that she has been limping since the injury and it has not improved. She denies hearing a pop. She states that she rates her pain as a 4 out of 10. She states that she started off with a 4, but it has gotten worse. She states it is a throbbing pain. She states that she has been taking Tylenol as she cannot take ibuprofen due to an ulcer. She has wrapped her knee with kinesiology tape. She states that the Tylenol helps a little bit, but she cannot take it too often. She states that she has been taking it every 4 hours.\n\nREVIEW OF SYSTEMS\n\nMusculoskeletal: Reports left knee pain.\n\nPHYSICAL EXAM\n\nConstitutional: No fever, chills, or weight loss.\n\nMusculoskeletal: Examination of the left knee reveals 2+ edema and effusion. She has moderate range of motion. She has pain with flexion and extension of the knee. She has a negative Lachman\'s test and a negative valgus and varus test. She has no ecchymosis or erythema.\n\nRESULTS\n\nX-rays of the left knee were reviewed and were normal. No fractures or bony abnormalities were noted.\n\nASSESSMENT AND PLAN\n\nLeft Knee Pain\n\nMedical Collateral Ligament Strain\n\nPatient was advised to follow up in 2 weeks. She was advised to take tramadol 50 mg twice a day. She was advised to take Tylenol as needed for breakthrough pain. She was advised to ice the knee 3-4 times a day for 20-30 minutes. She was advised to wear a knee immobilizer for 1 week. She was advised to avoid running for 3 weeks. She was advised to follow up with the office if she has any worsening of her symptoms.\n\nMEDICATIONS\n\nTramadol 50 mg twice a day\nTylenol as needed for breakthrough pain }}<|eot_id|>\n\n###Reference Answer:\nHISTORY OF PRESENT ILLNESS\n\nAngela Powell is a pleasant 81-year-old female who presents to the clinic today for the evaluation of left knee pain. The onset of her pain began 1 week ago, when she was running around with her niece and fell. At the time of the injury, she heard a pop and believes that she twisted her knee. She reports that she has been limping since the injury. The patient locates her pain to the medial and lateral aspects of her knee. She rates her pain level as a 4 out of 10, however this is increasing. Her pain is described as a throbbing pain. The patient has been taking Tylenol, which has provided mild and temporary relief, as well as wrapping her knee with kinesiology tape. She states that she is unable to take ibuprofen secondary to an ulcer.\n\nPHYSICAL EXAM\n\nMSK: Examination of the left knee: Edema and effusion noted. Moderate ROM. Pain with flexion and extension of the knee. Negative Lachman\'s. Negative valgus and varus stress test.\n\nRESULTS\n\n4 views of the left knee were taken. These reveal no evidence of any fractures or dislocations. No other abnormalities are noted.\n\nASSESSMENT\n\nLeft knee pain, possible MCL strain.\n\nPLAN\n\nAfter reviewing the patient\'s examination and radiographic findings today, I have discussed with the patient that her x-rays did not reveal any signs of a tear. I have recommended that we treat the patient conservatively. The plan is to place the patient in a knee immobilizer to provide increased support for the next week. I have also prescribed the patient tramadol 50 mg to treat her pain. She can take Tylenol as needed for breakthrough pain. I have also advised her to ice her left knee 3 to 4 times a day for 20 to 30 minutes at a time. I have advised her to refrain from running for the next 3 weeks. If her pain does not improve with the immobilizer, I will recommend obtaining an MRI.\n\n\n###Score Rubric:\n \n1. Hallucination:\n    - Does the summary note accurately and comprehensively reflect the doctor–patient dialogue and ground truth note?\n\n2. Critical Omissions:\n    - Does the summary note capture all essential medical facts from the doctor–patient dialogue and ground truth note?\n\n3. Professional Tone:\n    - Does the summary note maintain a consistently professional tone appropri